---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Brandmark from '../components/Brandmark.astro';
import { BOOKING_URL, CONTACT_EMAIL } from '../config';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About Phronimos | Custom Managed AI Agents"
  description="Phronimos designs, builds, and runs custom AI agents for owner-led businesses. The name is Ancient Greek for practical wisdom, and it sets the standard the work is judged by."
>
  <section class="section-tight page-head">
    <div class="container">
      <hr class="rule" data-reveal />
      <h1 data-reveal style="--reveal-i: 1">About Phronimos</h1>
      <p class="lede" data-reveal style="--reveal-i: 2">
        <strong>Phronimos designs, builds, and runs custom AI agents for owner-led businesses.</strong>
        The bet behind the name is that judgment about which work to automate matters more than
        the technology used to automate it.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section name-story">
    <div class="container name-grid">
      <div class="mark-block" data-reveal>
        <Brandmark size={120} variant="dark" />
      </div>
      <div class="name-copy" data-reveal style="--reveal-i: 1">
        <h2>The name</h2>
        <p>
          Phronimos comes from the Ancient Greek <em>φρόνιμος</em>: prudent, practically wise,
          exercising sound judgment. It is the adjectival form of <em>phronesis</em>, Aristotle's
          word for practical wisdom. Knowing not just what is true, but what to do.
        </p>
        <p>
          The letter that opens the name, phi, is also the symbol of the golden ratio: the
          classical proportion associated with balance and sound structure. That is the brand's
          argument in a single glyph. Intelligence in the right proportion, applied with judgment.
        </p>
        <p class="muted pronounce">Pronounced FRAH-nih-mos.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section why">
    <div class="container why-inner">
      <h2 data-reveal>Why Phronimos exists</h2>
      <div class="prose" data-reveal style="--reveal-i: 1">
        <p>
          Phronimos was founded on a simple conviction: most businesses are full of capable people
          spending their days on work that is repetitive, mechanical, or purely mechanical
          coordination. The opportunity is to design durable solutions to those operational
          problems, so owners and their teams get their attention back for work that needs a person.
        </p>
        <p>
          That motivation sets the center of gravity. The interesting object here is the problem:
          the bottleneck, the handoff that keeps failing, the task that quietly consumes a day a
          week. The technology is whatever turns out to solve it, and a simpler answer usually wins.
        </p>
        <p>
          This is why the work values judgment over novelty and reliability over hype. The measure
          is whether the business runs better six months later.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section principles">
    <div class="container">
      <h2 data-reveal>Operating principles</h2>
      <ul class="principles-grid">
        <li data-reveal>
          <h3>Practical wisdom first.</h3>
          <p class="muted">
            Every system is judged by the quality of its decisions. If a simpler approach produces
            a sounder result, that is the more intelligent approach.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li data-reveal style="--reveal-i: 1">
          <h3>Reliability is the product.</h3>
          <p class="muted">
            An agent that works most of the time costs more attention than it saves, because
            somebody has to check it. We optimize for consistent, supervisable execution over
            peak-case brilliance.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li data-reveal style="--reveal-i: 2">
          <h3>Solve the business problem.</h3>
          <p class="muted">
            Start from the bottleneck and work backward. Whether the answer turns out to be an
            agent, a rewritten process, or one fewer meeting is a detail of the diagnosis.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li data-reveal style="--reveal-i: 3">
          <h3>Capacity, not headcount math.</h3>
          <p class="muted">
            Success looks like a team taking on work it could not previously absorb, with the same
            people doing the parts that need a person.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section staffing">
    <div class="container staffing-inner">
      <h2 data-reveal>How engagements are staffed</h2>
      <div class="prose" data-reveal style="--reveal-i: 1">
        <p>
          Every Phronimos engagement runs end to end with the same practitioner: the audit call, the
          build, and the monitoring afterward. There is no handoff between the person who scopes the
          work and the person who does it.
        </p>
        <p>
          That is why the practice deliberately holds one or two active builds at a time. Capacity is
          the constraint that protects the quality, and it is the reason the pattern that makes
          buyers wary of this category, where a senior person sells and a junior person delivers,
          has nowhere to happen here.
        </p>
        <p>
          The monitoring, failure alerts, and documented playbooks that ship with every client
          operator also run the Phronimos delivery pipeline. The discipline sold to clients is the
          discipline the business runs on, which is the only version of that claim worth making.
        </p>
        <p class="staffing-links">
          Questions about how an engagement runs? Write to
          <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a>, or read
          <a href="/security/">how system access is handled</a>.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section-tight honest">
    <div class="container">
      <h2 data-reveal>Who Phronimos is a poor fit for</h2>
      <ul class="honest-list">
        <li data-reveal>
          <p>
            Businesses looking for the cheapest possible automation. A single Zapier consultant will
            cost less and may be the right call.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li data-reveal style="--reveal-i: 1">
          <p>
            Teams whose core records live in systems nobody can export from. Agents need reliable
            inputs, and the audit will say so plainly if yours are not there yet.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li data-reveal style="--reveal-i: 2">
          <p>
            Companies wanting one large system built and then handed over with no ongoing
            relationship. That model exists elsewhere and is honestly served elsewhere.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p class="muted honest-note" data-reveal>
        The audit ends in a verdict, and sometimes the verdict is that the timing is wrong. That
        answer is worth the fee too.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section final-cta">
    <div class="container">
      <h2 class="display" data-reveal>See it applied to your operations.</h2>
      <div class="cta-actions" data-reveal style="--reveal-i: 1">
        <a href={BOOKING_URL} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="btn btn-primary">Book the AI Workflow Audit</a>
        <span class="muted">or write to <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a></span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .page-head h1 {
    margin-top: var(--space-6);
    max-width: 24ch;
  }

  .name-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 3fr) minmax(0, 9fr);
    gap: clamp(32px, 5vw, 72px);
    align-items: start;
  }

  .mark-block {
    display: flex;
    justify-content: center;
    padding-top: var(--space-3);
  }

  .name-copy p {
    margin-top: var(--space-4);
    max-width: 62ch;
  }

  .pronounce {
    font-size: 15px;
  }

  .why {
    border-top: 1px solid var(--line);
    background: var(--surface-raised);
  }

  .why-inner .prose {
    margin-top: var(--space-5);
  }

  .principles-grid {
    list-style: none;
    padding: 0;
    margin: var(--space-7) 0 0;
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
    gap: var(--space-7) var(--space-8);
  }

  .principles-grid h3 {
    font-size: 19px;
  }

  .principles-grid .muted {
    margin-top: var(--space-2);
    font-size: 15.5px;
    max-width: 46ch;
  }

  .staffing {
    border-top: 1px solid var(--line);
  }

  .staffing-inner .prose {
    margin-top: var(--space-5);
  }

  .staffing-links {
    margin-top: var(--space-5);
    font-size: 15.5px;
  }

  /* Naming a poor fit is a premium signal, and the only one available before
     there are case studies. Kept plain on purpose. */
  .honest {
    border-top: 1px solid var(--line);
    background: var(--surface-raised);
  }

  .honest-list {
    list-style: none;
    padding: 0;
    margin: var(--space-6) 0 0;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--line);
    max-width: 72ch;
  }

  .honest-list li {
    padding-block: var(--space-5);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line);
  }

  .honest-list p {
    max-width: 64ch;
  }

  .honest-note {
    margin-top: var(--space-5);
    max-width: 56ch;
    font-size: 15.5px;
  }

  .final-cta .display {
    max-width: 18ch;
  }

  .cta-actions {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-items: center;
    gap: var(--space-5);
    margin-top: var(--space-7);
  }

  @media (max-width: 860px) {
    .name-grid,
    .principles-grid {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    }

    .mark-block {
      justify-content: flex-start;
    }
  }
</style>
